group: D6h
formula: C6H6
construction: its two sets of equivalent atoms
headline: 
source: MO levels: RHF, def2-SVP · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation in eV · electrons: C6 24, drawn 24; H6 6, drawn 6; model and calculation: 30 drawn + 0 folded (0 levels) = 30 · labels in D<sub>6h</sub>\n5 high empty levels drawn at the top (not to scale); 7 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (C6 and H6). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments: above +0.02 bonding, below -0.02 antibonding (*), otherwise non-bonding. A bonding and an antibonding level of the same degeneracy that are the in-phase and out-of-phase combinations of the same fragment orbitals are joined as partners and share a colour; a level without such a partner keeps its own classification.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. Beside each matched level, "overlap" is |<model MO|calculated MO>| (a number from 0 to 1); values below 0.80 are counted in the note under the diagram. Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe order of some empty levels above the LUMO differs between the model and the calculation. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 30** (C6 24, H6 6).\n\n**Construction labels.** sigma, pi and delta are taken relative to each bond from the centre to a ligand atom: sigma = no nodal plane containing the bond axis, pi = one, delta = two (s and a p orbital along the bond have none, a p orbital across it one, a d orbital as many as its m about the bond). For a level made of several ligand orbitals the share on each type is averaged over the bonds, and the label is the dominant one; the hover text gives the split. * marks the out-of-phase combination of the leading fragment orbitals, nb a level with under 5 % on the other fragment. For a diatomic the levels of each sigma/pi, g/u class are named in the textbook order (2s before 2p, bonding before antibonding) whatever their s/p mixing.\n\n**Effect on the bond.** The hover text gives dε/dR, the change of the model level's energy when the bond is stretched, from the model run at the bond length plus and minus 0.02 Å (a central atom: all its bonds together; two fragments: their distance, both rigid). A level that rises on stretching is bonding and strengthens the bond; one that falls weakens it; under 0.5 eV/Å is called little effect.\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\n**Caveats.** 5 high empty levels drawn at the top (not to scale); 7 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital. Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the diagram, marked with an up arrow, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\nDrawn as its two sets of equivalent atoms. This molecule has no central atom, so there is no central-atom diagram; combining fragments of it instead needs a subgroup and a choice.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in D6h; the code reported none.
verdict: differs The model puts 2b1u below 3a1g, the model puts 3e1u below 1b2u, the model puts 1a2u below 1b2u; the calculation has those the other way round (3 of 55 pairs of the 11 levels judged).
